.log:19023:+    - Inside the ruler-defined rectangular partitions, some cells contain a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19027:+    - Parse the top and left rulers, then inspect every internal partition.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19029:+    - Group cropped objects by their top-ruler segment color, preserving left-to-right\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19030:+      ruler order; within each group, stack objects in top-to-bottom row-ruler order\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19037:+    - The edge rulers define a two-dimensional address system; the output ignores\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19039:+      ruler colors rather than by their original absolute positions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19044:+    assert height >= 3 and width >= 3, \"expected rulers plus an internal area\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19053:+    assert len(col_colors) == len(set(col_colors)), \"column ruler colors must be unique\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19054:+    assert len(row_colors) == len(set(row_colors)), \"row ruler colors must be unique\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19229:+    ruler_colors = random.sample(range(1, 10), rows + cols)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19230:+    col_colors = ruler_colors[:cols]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19231:+    row_colors = ruler_colors[cols:]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19232:+    object_palette = [c for c in range(1, 10) if c not in ruler_colors]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/codegen_gpt55_xhigh_1__codegen_prompts__3.log:19281:+    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid ruler-stack input\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/run_20260618_122058_gpt55_xhigh_missing/17__codegen_prompts__4.log:37:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1030756833/17/des.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"<new_puzzle>\\n- `description`：根据模板区域内已有碎片的颜色，在颜色图例中查找对应的填充图案，再将图案缩放后嵌入模板的空白腔体。\\n\\n- `Input structure`：\\n  - 背景为 black。\\n  - 网格上方有一排颜色图例：每个图例由一个单色方块（2×2 或 3×3）紧邻右侧一个同尺寸的小图案组成，图案由该颜色和 black 组成的二值掩码构成。图例之间用至少一列 black 分隔。\\n  - 网格下方有一个较大的 grey 边框矩形（模板），模板内部被 grey 隔墙分成若干大小不等的矩形腔体。\\n  - 每个腔体中恰好有一个单像素的前景色标记点（非 black 非 grey），颜色与某个图例方块颜色一致。其余腔体像素为 black。\\n\\n- `Rule`：\\n  - 对模板内的每个矩形腔体：\\n    1. 读取腔体中唯一标记点的颜色 C。\\n    2. 在上方图例中找到方块颜色为 C 的图例，提取其右侧的二值掩码图案。\\n    3. 将该图案按整数倍缩放（块缩放），使缩放后的图案恰好填满腔体的宽和高（腔体尺寸一定是图案尺寸的整数倍）。\\n    4. 将缩放后的图案绘入腔体：掩码前景像素用颜色 C 绘制，掩码背景像素保持 black。\\n  - 标记点本身被覆盖，不保留。模板边框和隔墙保持 grey 不变。\\n\\n- `Key insight`：单像素标记点不是绘制内容——它是索引键，用于在图例中查找对应图案；腔体面积与图案面积的比值隐含了缩放倍数，解题者需同时推断\\\"颜色→图案映射\\\"和\\\"腔体→缩放倍数\\\"两层关系。\\n</new_puzzle>\\n\\n<new_puzzle>\\n- `description`：将散布在网格中的彩色形状按照各自箭头指示的方向滑动，在碰到同色边框通道的死端时停止，然后用形状的轮廓颜色填充通道中被形状覆盖区域以外的剩余空间。\\n\\n- `Input structure`：\\n  - 背景为 black。\\n  - 网格中有若干条单色边框通道（每条通道是由某种颜色的像素围成的长矩形管道，内部为 black，一端封闭、另一端开口）。每条通道的颜色各不相同。\\n  - 每条通道的开口端附近有一个小的 4-连通彩色形状（2×2 至 3×4 大小），形状颜色与某条通道颜色相同。\\n  - 每个形状内部恰好有一个单像素的箭头标记像素（用 red 表示），位于形状边缘的某一侧（上/下/左/右），指示滑动方向：red 像素在形状的哪一侧，形状就向该侧滑动。\\n\\n- `Rule`：\\n  1. 识别每个彩色形状及其内部 red 标记像素的位置（形状上边缘→向上滑，形状下边缘→向下滑，形状左边缘→向左滑，形状右边缘→向右滑）。\\n  2. 将形状沿该方向滑动，进入与其同色的通道开口，一直滑到形状的前缘紧贴通道封闭端为止。\\n  3. 滑动后，形状像素（包括 red 标记）保留在新位置，颜色全部统一为通道颜色（red 标记被覆盖为通道颜色）。\\n  4. 通道内部除了被形状占据的像素外，其余 black 空间保持 black 不变。形状原始位置恢复为 black。\\n\\n- `Key insight`：解题需要两层推理——第一层是从 red 标记在形状中的相对位置推断运动方向（而非从通道朝向推断），第二层是通过颜色匹配确定每个形状归属哪条通道。箭头标记位置编码方向的方式不直观，增加了机制的隐蔽性。\\n</new_puzzle>\\n\\n<new_puzzle>\\n- `description`：在输入网格中找到被外框包围的若干独立区域，按区域内\\\"种子行\\\"的颜色顺序逐层向上堆叠交替色带，生成彩色条纹塔。\\n\\n- `Input structure`：\\n  - 背景为 black。\\n  - 网格中有若干个不同大小的矩形 grey 边框（边框宽度为 1 像素），彼此不重叠。\\n  - 每个边框内部底部有 1 至 3 行连续的\\\"种子行\\\"，每行是一种纯色（非 black 非 grey），不同行颜色不同。种子行之上的内部空间全部为 black。\\n  - 边框的内部宽度均为奇数。\\n\\n- `Rule`：\\n  1. 对每个 grey 边框区域，从底部读取种子行序列（从下到上记为颜色 C1, C2, C3…）。\\n  2. 从种子行顶端开始，向上交替循环绘制色带：依次用 C1, C2, C3, C1, C2, C3… 逐行填充，直到填满边框内部剩余空间。\\n  3. 同时，每画完一个完整循环（C1→C2→C3），色带的宽度从边框内部满宽开始，左右各缩进 1 像素（即宽度减少 2），形成阶梯状收窄的\\\"塔\\\"形。\\n  4. 当宽度缩减至 1 像素时，继续用循环颜色填充单像素列，直到触及边框顶部内壁。\\n  - 种子行本身保持不变。\\n\\n- `Key insight`：解题者不仅要发现颜色循环的规律，还要注意到每完成一轮颜色循环后宽度递减——这个\\\"循环周期\\\"与\\\"几何收窄\\\"之间的绑定关系是核心难点。仅从单个样例可能会误以为是简单的逐行缩窄，需要多个样例（种子行数量不同）才能确认宽度缩减的触发条件是颜色循环完成而非固定行数。\\n</new_puzzle>\\n\\n<new_puzzle>\\n- `description`：在棋盘状网格中，根据每个彩色碎片所在象限确定旋转角度，再根据碎片自身孔洞数量选择目标画布区域进行放置。\\n\\n- `Input structure`：\\n  - 背景为 black。\\n  - 网格被一条水平 grey 线和一条垂直 grey 线分成四个象限（左上、右上、左下、右下）。\\n  - 每个象限中散布着 1 至 3 个小型彩色碎片（4-连通的前景对象，每个碎片为单一颜色，大小约 3×3 至 5×5）。部分碎片内部有 1 或 2 个 black 孔洞像素（被碎片完全包围的 black 像素）。\\n  - 网格右下角（右下象限之外、紧邻网格底部和右侧边缘）有一排小型 grey 边框方格，数量等于所有碎片总数。方格从左到右排列，每个方格内部大小足以容纳最大碎片的旋转后外接矩形。方格分成两行：上面一行放置有 0 个孔洞的碎片，下面一行放置有 1 个及以上孔洞的碎片。\\n\\n- `Rule`：\\n  1. 象限决定旋转角度：左上象限的碎片不旋转（0°）、右上顺时针旋转 90°、右下旋转 180°、左下逆时针旋转 90°。\\n  2. 孔洞数量决定目标行：碎片内部 black 孔洞数为 0 的放入上行方格，孔洞数 ≥ 1 的放入下行方格。\\n  3. 在各行内，碎片按原始位置从左到右的顺序依次放入方格（先按象限左上→右上→左下→右下的阅读顺序，象限内再按碎片中心的列坐标从小到大排列）。\\n  4. 旋转后的碎片居中放入目标方格，保持原颜色。原象限中碎片的原始位置清除为 black。\\n\\n- `Key insight`：解题包含三层需要独立归纳的规则——（1）象限位置编码旋转角度而非颜色或大小；（2）孔洞数量作为分类键决定目标行，而非碎片颜色；（3）放置顺序遵循阅读序。三层规则交织在同一视觉场景中，需要多个样例逐层剥离。\\n</new_puzzle>\\n\\n<new_puzzle>\\n- `description`：将网格中散落的单色线段碎片识别为\\\"笔画\\\"，按笔画方向和长度匹配到对应的彩色锚点对，然后用锚点颜色重新着色并连接锚点对。\\n\\n- `Input structure`：\\n  - 背景为 black。\\n  - 网格中有若干对彩色锚点：每对由两个相同颜色的单像素点组成（如两个 blue 点、两个 green 点等），不同颜色对之间颜色各不相同。每对锚点要么在同一行（水平对齐）、要么在同一列（垂直对齐）。\\n  - 网格中还散布着若干条 grey 线段碎片：每条线段是一条连续的水平或垂直的 grey 像素序列（长度 ≥ 2），各线段互不连通。\\n  - 每条 grey 线段的长度恰好等于某对同色锚点之间的间距（即锚点对之间空格数 + 1），且方向也相同（水平线段匹配水平锚点对，垂直线段匹配垂直锚点对）。每对锚点恰好匹配一条线段。\\n\\n- `Rule`：\\n  1. 对每对同色锚点，计算它们之间的间距和方向（水平/垂直）。\\n  2. 在所有 grey 线段中找到方向相同且长度等于该间距的线段（匹配唯一）。\\n  3. 删除该 grey 线段的原始位置。\\n  4. 在两个锚点之间绘制一条连接线，颜色为锚点颜色，线段覆盖两锚点之间（含锚点本身）的所有像素。\\n  - 所有锚点对处理完毕后，网格中不应残留任何 grey 像素。\\n\\n- `Key insight`：grey 线段本身的位置完全无关——它们只是\\\"长度和方向\\\"的编码载体。解题者容易被线段的空间位置误导，试图将线段平移到锚点处；实际上需要忽略线段位置，仅用其长度和方向与锚点对进行抽象匹配。这种\\\"空间位置是噪声、几何属性才是信号\\\"的模式是核心隐蔽点。\\n</new_puzzle>\\n\\n<new_puzzle>\\n- `description`：识别网格中嵌套方框的层级结构，根据每层方框内角落标记的颜色，对最内层的种子图案逐层施加不同的变换（翻转或旋转），从内向外展开输出。\\n\\n- `Input structure`：\\n  - 背景为 black。\\n  - 网格中心有一个小的彩色种子图案（约 3×3 至 5×5，由多种颜色组成的非对称图案）。\\n  - 种子图案外面嵌套着 2 至 4 层同心矩形空心边框，每层边框颜色为 grey。相邻边框之间间隔至少 1 像素的 black 空间。\\n  - 每层边框的左上角内侧（边框内壁的左上角位置）有一个单像素彩色标记：\\n    - red 标记表示水平翻转。\\n    - blue 标记表示垂直翻转。\\n    - green 标记表示顺时针旋转 90°。\\n    - yellow 标记表示旋转 180°。\\n\\n- `Rule`：\\n  1. 从最内层边框开始，读取其左上角标记颜色，对种子图案执行对应变换，得到第一次变换结果。\\n  2. 向外移动到下一层边框，读取该层标记颜色，对上一步结果再执行对应变换。\\n  3. 重复直到最外层边框的变换完成。\\n  4. 输出为最终变换后的种子图案（仅输出图案本身，裁剪到图案的紧凑边界框），不包含任何边框或标记。\\n\\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要发现三件事：（1）角落标记的颜色不是装饰而是变换指令；（2）变换是从内到外逐层累积的，顺序不同结果不同（如先水平翻转再旋转 ≠ 先旋转再水平翻转）；（3）输出是纯净的变换后图案，所有框架结构都是指令编码而非输出内容。嵌套层数与变换复合的非交换性使得仅从一个样例难以区分各标记的含义。\\n</new_puzzle>\\n\\n<new_puzzle>\\n- `description`：在网格中找到每个彩色区域的\\\"影子投射方向\\\"（由该区域附带的单像素方向标记决定），然后沿该方向在背景上绘制该区域的灰色投影，投影长度等于区域中同色连通像素的数量。\\n\\n- `Input structure`：\\n  - 背景为 black。\\n  - 网格中有若干个不重叠的彩色 4-连通区域（每个区域为单一颜色，颜色互不相同，大小约 3 至 8 个像素）。\\n  - 每个彩色区域在其外侧恰好有一个紧邻的 maroon 单像素标记点，位于区域边界框外的上、下、左或右某一侧（maroon 点与区域有且仅有一个像素相邻）。\\n\\n- `Rule`：\\n  1. 对每个彩色区域，找到其紧邻的 maroon 标记点。标记点相对于区域的方向即为投影方向（标记在区域上方→投影向上，标记在右方→投影向右，以此类推）。\\n  2. 数出该彩色区域的像素数量 N。\\n  3. 对区域中的每个像素，沿投影方向绘制长度为 N 的 grey 线段（不含起始像素本身，从该像素沿方向走 N 步）。\\n  4. 投影的 grey 像素不覆盖任何已有的彩色区域像素或 maroon 标记——若投影路径上遇到非 black 像素则该像素跳过不画（投影继续延伸，总步数仍为 N）。\\n  5. 原始彩色区域和 maroon 标记保持不变。\\n\\n- `Key insight`：投影方向不是固定的也不是由形状朝向决定的，而是由外部 maroon 标记的相对位置编码；投影长度不是由区域的宽或高决定，而是由区域面积（像素数）决定。解题者需要分别发现方向编码机制和长度编码机制这两层独立规则。\\n</new_puzzle>\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/run_20260618_122058_gpt55_xhigh_missing/17__codegen_prompts__4.log:41:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1030756833/17/prompt.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\\n\\n每个参考题描述包含：\\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\\n\\n参考题目如下：\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"1\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"2b9ef948\\\" lines=\\\"51-65\\\">\\n<description>\\nMove the yellow 3x3 frame by the displacement encoded by the gray path, then draw its diagonal yellow corner rays.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- The background is black.\\n- One 3x3 yellow frame has a non-yellow control-color center.\\n- A second control-color pixel is connected through gray path pixels to a lone yellow endpoint.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Use the vector from the second control-color pixel to the lone yellow endpoint as the frame's translation.\\n- Fill the whole output with the control color.\\n- Draw the translated yellow frame and extend yellow diagonal rays outward from its four corners to the grid edge.\\n\\nKey insight: The gray path's turns are irrelevant; its two colored endpoints encode the net displacement to apply to the frame.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"2\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"522fdd07\\\" lines=\\\"8-23\\\">\\n<description>\\nCycle each solid odd-sided square to the next smaller centered square, wrapping a single pixel to a 9x9 square.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\\n- Every foreground object is a separated 4-connected monochrome solid square.\\n- Square side lengths are odd and belong to the cycle 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Clear the canvas to the background.\\n- Keep each square's color and center.\\n- Replace side length n by n - 2 when n > 1; replace side length 1 by side length 9.\\n\\nKey insight:\\n- The visible operation is on square size, not color: side lengths cycle 1 -> 9 -> 7 -> 5 -> 3 -> 1.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"3\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"42f83767\\\" lines=\\\"23-39\\\">\\n<description>\\nExpand the lower color grid by replacing each pixel with its matching top stencil.\\n\\nInput structure\\nThe input has a top legend and a lower dense color grid, separated by blank rows.\\nThe legend contains left-to-right 2x2 color swatches and, to their right,\\nthe same number of square neutral-color stencil masks.\\n\\nRule\\nPair the swatches and stencil masks by left-to-right order. For every pixel in\\nthe lower color grid, paste the paired stencil mask as one output block,\\nrecoloring the mask pixels with that pixel's color and leaving mask holes as bg.\\n\\nKey insight\\nThe lower grid is not copied directly; it is an index grid whose colors select\\nthe stencil shape from the legend, so each input pixel becomes one square block.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"4\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"8a6d367c\\\" lines=\\\"52-67\\\">\\n<description>\\nComplete the framed area by expanding the unique upper template that contains the existing seed pixels.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- A teal background contains small colored template masks above a larger rectangular frame.\\n- The frame encloses one fill color arranged as a partial block-scaled copy of one template.\\n- The frame interior dimensions are an integer square scale of the matching template bbox.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Crop to the rectangular frame.\\n- Identify the unique upper template whose block-scaled mask covers every existing fill pixel inside the frame.\\n- Reset the frame interior to background and paint the whole scaled mask with the fill color.\\n\\nKey insight:\\nThe lower colored pixels are seed cells that reveal which upper template should be completed inside the frame.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"5\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"458e3a53\\\" lines=\\\"19-32\\\">\\n<description>\\nExtract the compact table of solid cells hidden inside a separator lattice.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The grid has full-width and full-height bars of one separator color, dividing it into rectangular cells.\\n- Most cells contain a repeating multicolor pattern, while a contiguous rectangle of cells is solid monochrome.\\n\\nRule\\n- Split the input into lattice cells using the full separator rows and columns.\\n- Keep the cells whose full interior is one color, crop to their occupied rectangle, and output those colors.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The periodic pattern is noise; only completely solid lattice cells encode the reduced output grid.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"6\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"b10624e5\\\" lines=\\\"24-39\\\">\\n<description>\\nReplicate a motif across quadrants with scaling and reflection keyed to red anchor blocks.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- Grid divided into four quadrants by a full-row and full-column divider of a single non-bg color.\\n- Each quadrant contains a red (2) square anchor block of varying size.\\n- The top-left quadrant also has a colored motif attached to its red anchor.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Use the top-left quadrant's motif as the source pattern.\\n- For each other quadrant, scale the motif by the ratio of that quadrant's red block size to the source red block size.\\n- Reflect horizontally for right-side quadrants, vertically for bottom quadrants (both for bottom-right).\\n- Align the scaled/reflected motif so its red region overlaps the target anchor, then stamp non-bg pixels.\\n\\nKey insight: Red block size encodes the scale factor; quadrant position encodes the reflection axes.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"7\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"df9fd884\\\" lines=\\\"33-47\\\">\\n<description>\\nMove the colored object from the lower bracket lane to the upper bracket lane and drop it to the bottom edge.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The background is the dominant color.\\n- One frame color forms exactly two 4-connected bracket objects: a top-edge bracket open downward and a bottom-edge bracket open upward.\\n- One other 4-connected foreground object sits in the lower bracket's interior column span.\\n\\nRule\\n- Remove the movable object from its original cells.\\n- Keep its shape and color unchanged.\\n- Paste it with its bbox columns aligned to the top bracket's interior columns and with its bbox bottom aligned to the grid bottom.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The lower bracket identifies the object's source lane; the upper bracket provides the target lane, and the final vertical placement is bottom-aligned.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"8\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"fe45cba4\\\" lines=\\\"72-89\\\">\\n<description>\\nMove the left-edge fragment into its same-colored anchor fragment to complete a solid rectangle.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\\n- Exactly one foreground color is split into two 4-connected objects: one\\n  touches the left edge, and the other is an anchor away from the left edge.\\n- The translated left-edge object and the anchor can form one filled rectangle.\\nRule\\n- Find the unique split color whose left-edge object can be translated onto\\n  background cells beside the other same-colored object so their union is a\\n  solid axis-aligned rectangle.\\n- Clear the left-edge object's original cells to the background.\\n- Paint that object at the unique completing offset, preserving all other cells.\\nKey insight\\n- The active color is identified by object structure and rectangle completion,\\n  not by a fixed color id; the same-colored anchor object stays in place.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"9\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"45a5af55\\\" lines=\\\"5-18\\\">\\n<description>\\nBuild nested square frames from a stack of horizontal color stripes.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- Full-width horizontal stripes of distinct non-bg colors, stacked vertically.\\n- Each stripe has uniform color and variable height (1-4 rows).\\n\\nRule:\\n- Read stripes bottom to top.\\n- Bottom stripe becomes the center solid square with side length = stripe height.\\n- Each subsequent stripe wraps the current shape with a square frame of thickness = stripe height, in that stripe's color.\\n\\nKey insight: Stripe height doubles as both frame thickness and (for the innermost) center-square side length; output dimensions are fully determined by the stripe heights.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"10\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"5dbc8537\\\" lines=\\\"10-23\\\">\\n<description>\\nFill rectangular cavities in a template shape by placing source sprites into matching holes.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = most common color. A dominant \\\"base\\\" color forms a large frame/template with rectangular gaps.\\n- Small colored sprites sit outside the template region.\\n\\nRule:\\n- The base color (largest connected component excluding bg) defines the template bbox.\\n- Each rectangular hole inside the template matches exactly one outside sprite by size.\\n- Place each sprite into its matching cavity; output is the filled template bbox.\\n\\nKey insight: Sprite bounding boxes match cavity dimensions exactly — it's a jigsaw-fit puzzle where each piece has a unique slot.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"11\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"985ae207\\\" lines=\\\"82-97\\\">\\n<description>\\nRepeat each 3x3 control-center sprite toward the matching solid rectangle until a copy first overlaps that rectangle.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The background is the dominant color.\\n- Each sprite is a 3x3 foreground object whose eight border pixels have one color and whose center has a different control color.\\n- For every control color, there is exactly one separate solid rectangular object of that color.\\n\\nRule\\n- Match each sprite to the unique solid rectangle whose color equals the sprite center.\\n- Copy the whole 3x3 sprite in the cardinal direction of that rectangle, stepping by three cells each time.\\n- Stop after pasting the first copy whose 3x3 bbox overlaps the rectangle; copied pixels overwrite the rectangle there.\\n\\nKey insight\\nThe center pixel is not the paint color: it is a destination key, while the entire 3x3 sprite is the motif tiled along the straight path.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"12\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"3a25b0d8\\\" lines=\\\"9-22\\\">\\n<description>\\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\\n\\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"13\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"7c66cb00\\\" lines=\\\"9-24\\\">\\n<description>\\nDock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = most common color.\\n- Top area: small two-tone sprites (outer color + inner color).\\n- Below: horizontal bars spanning most of the row, each with a fill color and border color on edges.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Match each sprite to the bar whose fill color equals the sprite's outer color.\\n- Place the sprite at its original x-position, aligned to the bar's bottom.\\n- Recolor: sprite outer → bar border, sprite inner → bar fill.\\n- Erase original sprites from the top area.\\n\\nKey insight: Color matching is inverted — the sprite's dominant color identifies its target bar, then gets swapped to the bar's accent color.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"14\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"446ef5d2\\\" lines=\\\"8-20\\\">\\n<description>\\nAssemble scattered rectangular pieces into a rectangle at an L-marker corner.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = most common color. Non-bg rectangular pieces scattered on the grid.\\n- 3-pixel L-marker (2x2 minus one cell) tags the corner; the gap is the corner cell.\\n\\nRule:\\n- rect_col = most common color across all piece pixels; all outer rectangle edges must be rect_col.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/run_20260618_122058_gpt55_xhigh_missing/9__codegen_prompts__7.log:51:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_26\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1030756833/9/prompt.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\\n\\n每个参考题描述包含：\\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\\n\\n参考题目如下：\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"1\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"8886d717\\\" lines=\\\"43-63\\\">\\n<description>\\nRepair markers embedded in the main object and copy the remaining markers one pixel toward the solid edge.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- A mostly background grid contains one solid non-background edge line.\\n- Apart from that edge and the background, there is one large main object\\n  color and a smaller marker color.\\n- Each marker is either embedded in the main object, touching it on at\\n  least three 4-neighbor sides, or isolated on the background.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Embedded markers are recolored to the main object color.\\n- Isolated background markers remain in place and also paint the adjacent\\n  background pixel in the direction of the solid edge line.\\n- All other pixels are unchanged.\\n\\nKey insight:\\nThe full edge line is a direction cue: it tells which way the background\\nmarkers extend, while markers surrounded by the main object are simply\\ndefects to be filled.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"2\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"b1986d4b\\\" lines=\\\"19-37\\\">\\n<description>\\nRender the filled-square object counts as compact nested stacks ordered by count rank.\\n\\nInput structure\\nA large noisy grid has a dominant background, scattered non-square noise, and\\nfilled monochrome square objects. Each square side length has exactly one\\nobject color, and side lengths form consecutive sizes.\\n\\nRule\\nIgnore non-square components. If the lowest square in the scene is of the\\nsmallest side length, treat that one smallest square as a distractor and do\\nnot count it. For count rank k from left to right, create one block from all\\nsquare sizes with at least k remaining objects; draw larger squares first and\\nsmaller squares over their upper-left corner, then append one background\\nseparator column.\\n\\nKey insight\\nThe output is a count histogram of square side lengths, but each histogram\\nslot is rendered as nested upper-left square masks using the side colors.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"3\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"b5bb5719\\\" lines=\\\"17-32\\\">\\n<description>\\nFill the background under a top-row red/grey span with a shrinking diagonal pattern that alternates red and grey.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- The grid has an orange background.\\n- All foreground pixels are red or grey and lie on the top row.\\n- The first and last top-row foreground pixels define an odd-width span.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Copy the input grid.\\n- For row r below the top, fill columns span_left + r through span_right - r.\\n- Each filled cell takes the top-row color r columns up-left, flipped red<->grey once per row step.\\n\\nKey insight:\\n- The output is the top-row span propagated down-right along diagonals, while the allowable region shrinks inward from both ends each row.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"4\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"7b3084d4\\\" lines=\\\"253-266\\\">\\n<description>\\nAssemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\\n- One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\\n- Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\\n- Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\\n\\nKey insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"5\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"269e22fb\\\" lines=\\\"46-60\\\">\\n<description>\\nComplete a visible crop into the shared 20x20 binary motif in its matching orientation.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- The grid is a two-color rectangular crop of one rotation/reflection of the same 20x20 motif.\\n- One color is the motif foreground and the other color is the motif background.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Try each input color as the foreground mask.\\n- Match that mask against every possible window in every dihedral orientation of the motif.\\n- Repaint the uniquely matched full 20x20 motif with the matched foreground/background colors.\\n\\nKey insight:\\n- The examples are partial windows of one canonical binary pattern; the local crop is distinctive enough to identify the full orientation and offset.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"6\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"494ef9d7\\\" lines=\\\"16-30\\\">\\n<description>\\nSlide each blue/teal or yellow/orange row pair together while preserving its left-to-right order.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The grid has a black background.\\n- Each nonempty row contains exactly two distinct foreground pixels.\\n- Blue/teal and yellow/orange row pairs are the moving pairs; other color pairs are distractors.\\n\\nRule\\n- For each moving pair, keep the left pixel fixed and move the right pixel left until it touches the left pixel.\\n- Clear the moved pixel's original location, and leave non-moving rows unchanged.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- Only same-hue-family pixel pairs attract horizontally; separated unrelated colors do not compact.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"7\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"8f215267\\\" lines=\\\"7-21\\\">\\n<description>\\nCount same-color patterns per frame and represent the tally as dots inside.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = most common color.\\n- Hollow rectangular frames in various colors.\\n- Small monochrome pattern sprites scattered outside frames.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Remove all small patterns from the grid.\\n- For each frame of color C, count how many patterns share color C.\\n- Draw that many dots along the frame's center row, spaced by 2, starting from the right.\\n\\nKey insight: Frames act as tally displays — scattered same-color patterns are \\\"absorbed\\\" and their count is shown as interior dots.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"8\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"52364a65\\\" lines=\\\"10-24\\\">\\n<description>\\nErase the two leftmost columns of every foreground object, leaving the rest of each object in place.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- A grid has one dominant background color and several monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects.\\n- Each foreground object spans at least two columns in its bounding box.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Find each same-color 4-connected foreground object.\\n- Within each object's own bounding box, set foreground pixels in the two leftmost columns to the background color.\\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\\n\\nKey insight:\\n- The deletion is measured from each object's local left edge, not from the grid edge or from a shared global column.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"9\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"d255d7a7\\\" lines=\\\"99-114\\\">\\n<description>\\nMove the patterned edge band to the opposite edge along every row or column whose band slice contains the trail color.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The dominant color is the background.\\n- The two foreground colors are a frequent trail color and a rarer marker color.\\n- All marker pixels lie nearest to one grid edge, and that edge contains trail-color runs that define the source-band thickness.\\n\\nRule\\n- Choose the source edge identified by the marker pixels.\\n- A row or column perpendicular to that edge moves if its source-band slice contains the trail color.\\n- Copy that slice unchanged to the opposite edge, erase the remaining trail cells to background, and keep marker-only lines fixed.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The marker color identifies the active edge, while the trail color identifies which parallel rows or columns carry an edge-band slice across the grid.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"10\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"c920a713\\\" lines=\\\"87-102\\\">\\n<description>\\nUntangle overlapping colored rectangular frames into concentric square frames ordered by their visible crossings.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The background contains several one-cell-thick axis-aligned rectangular frames, one color per frame.\\n- Frames overlap, and the visible color at an overlap records a directed crossing from the hidden frame to the visible frame.\\n- Each foreground color still has visible pixels on the perimeter of its full rectangular bounding box.\\n\\nRule\\n- Infer each color's full rectangular frame from the bounding box of its visible pixels.\\n- Add a directed edge A -> B when B is visible at a crossing of A and B's frame perimeters.\\n- Find the unique path that visits every frame color once, then render those colors as one-pixel-thick concentric square frames from outside to inside.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The output is not a crop of the input; the crossing graph gives a single frame order, and that order is redrawn as nested rings.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"11\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"332202d5\\\" lines=\\\"49-64\\\">\\n<description>\\nFill every row from the nearest marked horizontal bar, turning bar rows and color-boundary ties into separator rows.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- A dominant background fills the grid except for full-width horizontal bars and one vertical marker column.\\n- Outside the marker column, every row is monochrome: either background or a source bar color.\\n- Background rows share one marker color in the marker column; source bar rows share the other.\\n\\nRule\\n- Each non-source row takes the color of the nearest source bar, with that row's marker-column pixel set to the source-marker color.\\n- If a row is exactly midway between nearest bars of different colors, make the whole row the source-marker color.\\n- Each original source bar row becomes a source-marker row with the marker-column pixel changed to the background-row marker color.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The marked bars are horizontal seeds for a one-dimensional nearest-seed partition over rows; equal-distance rows are separators only when the competing seed colors differ.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/run_20260618_122058_gpt55_xhigh_missing/12__codegen_prompts__2.log:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '/\\\"'^def main(/,/''^def generate_input(/p; /''^def generate_input(/,$p'\\\"' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/58490d8a.py | sed -n '1,240p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Count colored objects and record tallies inside a legend box.\\n\\n    Input structure:\\n    - A black rectangular legend box with key-color pixels in its leftmost non-black column.\\n    - Outside the box, distinct objects of each key color are scattered across the grid.\\n\\n    Rule:\\n    - For each key color, count separate same-colored objects outside the legend box.\\n    - Output the legend box repainted: each key row gets one dot per object, spaced every other cell rightward from the key pixel.\\n\\n    Key insight: The legend is both the color index and the output canvas — object counts become dot sequences on the matching row.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n    H, W = g.shape\\n    _bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n\\n    black = 0\\n\\n    # STEP 1) Find the legend bbox: largest black CC whose bbox contains some non-black pixels.\\n    best = None  # (area, bbox)\\n    for coords in components_mask(g == int(black), connectivity=8):\\n        if coords.size == 0:\\n            continue\\n        b = bbox_coords(coords)\\n        sub = crop_bbox(g, b)\\n        if (sub != int(black)).any():\\n            area = int(coords.shape[0])\\n            cand = (area, tuple(map(int, b)))\\n            if best is None or cand[0] > best[0]:\\n                best = cand\\n\\n    assert not (best is None)\\n\\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = map(int, best[1])\\n    box = crop_bbox(g, (r0, c0, r1, c1))\\n    bh, bw = map(int, box.shape)\\n\\n    # STEP 2) Inside the legend bbox, find the key column:\\n    # leftmost column with any non-black pixels (the \\\"key pixels\\\").\\n    key_col = None\\n    for cc in range(bw):\\n        if (box[:, int(cc)] != int(black)).any():\\n            key_col = int(cc)\\n            break\\n    if key_col is None:\\n        return np.zeros_like(box)\\n\\n    keys = [(int(rr), int(box[int(rr), int(key_col)])) for rr in range(bh) if int(box[int(rr), int(key_col)]) != int(black)]\\n    keys.sort(key=lambda t: int(t[0]))\\n\\n    outside = np.ones((H, W), dtype=bool)\\n    outside[int(r0) : int(r1) + 1, int(c0) : int(c1) + 1] = False\\n    out = np.zeros((bh, bw), dtype=int)\\n\\n    # STEP 3/4) Count CCs of each key color outside the legend, then draw 1 dot per CC.\\n    for rr, col in keys:\\n        mask = (g == int(col)) & outside\\n        _labc, nc = label_mask(mask, connectivity=8)\\n        for i in range(int(nc)):\\n            cc = int(key_col + 2 * i)\\n            if 0 <= cc < bw:\\n                out[int(rr), int(cc)] = int(col)\\n\\n    return out\\n\\n# --------------- generator (o5x) ---------------\\n\\n# Palette and legend color choices.\\nCOLOR_POOL = tuple(range(1, 10))\\nBLACK = 0\\nDISTRACTOR_PROB = 0.5\\n\\n# Key rows and tally dimensions.\\nKEY_COUNT_RANGE = (2, 4)\\nTARGET_COUNT_RANGE = (1, 5)\\nKEY_ROW_FIRST = 1\\nKEY_ROW_STEP = 2\\nKEY_COLUMN = 1\\nTALLY_COL_STEP = 2\\nLEGEND_HEIGHT_TAIL = 1\\nLEGEND_WIDTH_TAIL = 1\\nLEGEND_SLACK_RANGE = (0, 3)\\n\\n# Canvas size and legend placement bounds.\\nGRID_HEIGHT_RANGE = (20, 25)\\nGRID_WIDTH_RANGE = (20, 25)\\nLEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN = 2\\n\\n# Sprite shape and placement constraints.\\nSPRITE_AREA_RANGE = (4, 7)\\nSPRITE_BBOXES = ((2, 2), (2, 3), (3, 2), (3, 3))\\nSPRITE_CONNECTIVITY = 8\\nSPRITE_PADDING = 1\\nSPRITE_PADDING_CONNECTIVITY = 8\\n\\n# Retry budget for rejection sampling.\\nGENERATION_ATTEMPTS = 300\\n\\n\\ndef _randint(bounds: tuple[int, int]) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return an inclusive randint from a configured (low, high) pair.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return random.randint(bounds[0], bounds[1])\\n\\n\\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\\ndef _valid_masks(h: int, w: int, area: int) -> tuple[np.ndarray, ...]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return connected masks that span all four sides of the sprite bbox.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    masks = []\\n    for cells in combinations(range(h * w), area):\\n        mask = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=bool)\\n        for cell in cells:\\n            mask[cell // w, cell % w] = True\\n        spans_edges = mask[0, :].any() and mask[-1, :].any() and mask[:, 0].any() and mask[:, -1].any()\\n        if not spans_edges:\\n            continue\\n        _, n = label_mask(mask, connectivity=SPRITE_CONNECTIVITY)\\n        if n == 1:\\n            masks.append(mask)\\n    return tuple(masks)\\n\\n\\ndef _make_sprite(color: int, bg: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Sample one connected same-color sprite drawn on the background color.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    area = _randint(SPRITE_AREA_RANGE)\\n    dims = []\\n    for h, w in SPRITE_BBOXES:\\n        if h * w >= area and _valid_masks(h, w, area):\\n            dims.append((h, w))\\n    h, w = random.choice(dims)\\n    mask = random.choice(_valid_masks(h, w, area))\\n    sprite = np.full((h, w), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n    sprite[mask] = int(color)\\n    return sprite\\n\\n\\ndef _sample_palette() -> tuple[int, list[int], int | None]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Choose background, key colors, and optional distractor color.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    colors = list(COLOR_POOL)\\n    bg = random.choice(colors)\\n\\n    k = _randint(KEY_COUNT_RANGE)\\n    remaining = [c for c in colors if c != bg]\\n    key_colors = random.sample(remaining, k)\\n    remaining = [c for c in remaining if c not in key_colors]\\n    distractor_color = random.choice(remaining) if remaining and random.random() < DISTRACTOR_PROB else None\\n    return bg, key_colors, distractor_color\\n\\n\\ndef _sample_counts(k: int) -> list[int]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Sample the target outside-component count for each key color.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return [_randint(TARGET_COUNT_RANGE) for _ in range(k)]\\n\\n\\ndef _legend_shape(k: int, max_count: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return legend height and width for the key count and largest tally.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    bh = KEY_ROW_STEP * k + LEGEND_HEIGHT_TAIL\\n    bw = TALLY_COL_STEP * max_count + LEGEND_WIDTH_TAIL + _randint(LEGEND_SLACK_RANGE)\\n    return bh, bw\\n\\n\\ndef _key_row(i: int) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Map a zero-based key index to its row inside the legend/output box.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return KEY_ROW_FIRST + KEY_ROW_STEP * i\\n\\n\\ndef _build_expected(key_colors: list[int], counts: list[int]) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Render the black output tally canvas implied by key colors and counts.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    max_count = max(counts)\\n    bh, bw = _legend_shape(len(key_colors), max_count)\\n\\n    expected = np.full((bh, bw), BLACK, dtype=int)\\n    for i, (color, count) in enumerate(zip(key_colors, counts)):\\n        rr = _key_row(i)\\n        for j in range(count):\\n            expected[rr, KEY_COLUMN + TALLY_COL_STEP * j] = int(color)\\n    return expected\\n\\n\\ndef _build_legend(shape: tuple[int, int], key_colors: list[int]) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return a black legend patch with only the key-column pixels filled.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    legend = np.full(shape, BLACK, dtype=int)\\n    for i, color in enumerate(key_colors):\\n        legend[_key_row(i), KEY_COLUMN] = int(color)\\n    return legend\\n\\n\\ndef _new_grid_with_legend(bg: int, legend: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray | None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Sample a grid and paste the legend away from all canvas edges.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    bh, bw = map(int, legend.shape)\\n    H = _randint(GRID_HEIGHT_RANGE)\\n    W = _randint(GRID_WIDTH_RANGE)\\n    min_h = bh + 2 * LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN\\n    min_w = bw + 2 * LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN\\n    if H < min_h or W < min_w:\\n        return None\\n\\n    grid = np.full((H, W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n    r0 = random.randint(LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN, H - bh - LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN)\\n    c0 = random.randint(LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN, W - bw - LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN)\\n    paste(grid, legend, r0, c0)\\n    return grid\\n\\n\\ndef _build_sprite_queue(\\n    key_colors: list[int],\\n    counts: list[int],\\n    distractor_color: int | None,\\n    bg: int,\\n) -> list[np.ndarray]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Create canonical key sprites, optional distractor sprite, and shuffle them.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    sprites = []\\n    for color, count in zip(key_colors, counts):\\n        sprite = _make_sprite(color, bg)\\n        for _ in range(count):\\n            sprites.append(sprite.copy())\\n    if distractor_color is not None:\\n        sprites.append(_make_sprite(distractor_color, bg))\\n    random.shuffle(sprites)\\n    return sprites\\n\\n\\ndef _place_sprites(grid: np.ndarray, sprites: list[np.ndarray], bg: int) -> None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Paste sprites into random non-touching free locations in queue order.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    for sprite in sprites:\\n        sr, sc = random_free_location(\\n            grid,\\n            sprite,\\n            transparent=bg,\\n            padding=SPRITE_PADDING,\\n            padding_connectivity=SPRITE_PADDING_CONNECTIVITY,\\n        )\\n        paste(grid, sprite, sr, sc, transparent=bg)\\n\\n\\ndef _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Run one output-first generation attempt and reject solver mismatches.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    bg, key_colors, distractor_color = _sample_palette()\\n    counts = _sample_counts(len(key_colors))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1030756833/_codegen_logs/run_20260618_122058_gpt55_xhigh_missing/11__codegen_prompts__7.log:7:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '/\\\"'^def main/,/''^def generate_input/p'\\\"' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/58490d8a.py && sed -n '/\\\"'^def generate_input/,$p'\\\"' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/58490d8a.py\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Count colored objects and record tallies inside a legend box.\\n\\n    Input structure:\\n    - A black rectangular legend box with key-color pixels in its leftmost non-black column.\\n    - Outside the box, distinct objects of each key color are scattered across the grid.\\n\\n    Rule:\\n    - For each key color, count separate same-colored objects outside the legend box.\\n    - Output the legend box repainted: each key row gets one dot per object, spaced every other cell rightward from the key pixel.\\n\\n    Key insight: The legend is both the color index and the output canvas — object counts become dot sequences on the matching row.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n    H, W = g.shape\\n    _bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n\\n    black = 0\\n\\n    # STEP 1) Find the legend bbox: largest black CC whose bbox contains some non-black pixels.\\n    best = None  # (area, bbox)\\n    for coords in components_mask(g == int(black), connectivity=8):\\n        if coords.size == 0:\\n            continue\\n        b = bbox_coords(coords)\\n        sub = crop_bbox(g, b)\\n        if (sub != int(black)).any():\\n            area = int(coords.shape[0])\\n            cand = (area, tuple(map(int, b)))\\n            if best is None or cand[0] > best[0]:\\n                best = cand\\n\\n    assert not (best is None)\\n\\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = map(int, best[1])\\n    box = crop_bbox(g, (r0, c0, r1, c1))\\n    bh, bw = map(int, box.shape)\\n\\n    # STEP 2) Inside the legend bbox, find the key column:\\n    # leftmost column with any non-black pixels (the \\\"key pixels\\\").\\n    key_col = None\\n    for cc in range(bw):\\n        if (box[:, int(cc)] != int(black)).any():\\n            key_col = int(cc)\\n            break\\n    if key_col is None:\\n        return np.zeros_like(box)\\n\\n    keys = [(int(rr), int(box[int(rr), int(key_col)])) for rr in range(bh) if int(box[int(rr), int(key_col)]) != int(black)]\\n    keys.sort(key=lambda t: int(t[0]))\\n\\n    outside = np.ones((H, W), dtype=bool)\\n    outside[int(r0) : int(r1) + 1, int(c0) : int(c1) + 1] = False\\n    out = np.zeros((bh, bw), dtype=int)\\n\\n    # STEP 3/4) Count CCs of each key color outside the legend, then draw 1 dot per CC.\\n    for rr, col in keys:\\n        mask = (g == int(col)) & outside\\n        _labc, nc = label_mask(mask, connectivity=8)\\n        for i in range(int(nc)):\\n            cc = int(key_col + 2 * i)\\n            if 0 <= cc < bw:\\n                out[int(rr), int(cc)] = int(col)\\n\\n    return out\\n\\n# --------------- generator (o5x) ---------------\\n\\n# Palette and legend color choices.\\nCOLOR_POOL = tuple(range(1, 10))\\nBLACK = 0\\nDISTRACTOR_PROB = 0.5\\n\\n# Key rows and tally dimensions.\\nKEY_COUNT_RANGE = (2, 4)\\nTARGET_COUNT_RANGE = (1, 5)\\nKEY_ROW_FIRST = 1\\nKEY_ROW_STEP = 2\\nKEY_COLUMN = 1\\nTALLY_COL_STEP = 2\\nLEGEND_HEIGHT_TAIL = 1\\nLEGEND_WIDTH_TAIL = 1\\nLEGEND_SLACK_RANGE = (0, 3)\\n\\n# Canvas size and legend placement bounds.\\nGRID_HEIGHT_RANGE = (20, 25)\\nGRID_WIDTH_RANGE = (20, 25)\\nLEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN = 2\\n\\n# Sprite shape and placement constraints.\\nSPRITE_AREA_RANGE = (4, 7)\\nSPRITE_BBOXES = ((2, 2), (2, 3), (3, 2), (3, 3))\\nSPRITE_CONNECTIVITY = 8\\nSPRITE_PADDING = 1\\nSPRITE_PADDING_CONNECTIVITY = 8\\n\\n# Retry budget for rejection sampling.\\nGENERATION_ATTEMPTS = 300\\n\\n\\ndef _randint(bounds: tuple[int, int]) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return an inclusive randint from a configured (low, high) pair.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return random.randint(bounds[0], bounds[1])\\n\\n\\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\\ndef _valid_masks(h: int, w: int, area: int) -> tuple[np.ndarray, ...]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return connected masks that span all four sides of the sprite bbox.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    masks = []\\n    for cells in combinations(range(h * w), area):\\n        mask = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=bool)\\n        for cell in cells:\\n            mask[cell // w, cell % w] = True\\n        spans_edges = mask[0, :].any() and mask[-1, :].any() and mask[:, 0].any() and mask[:, -1].any()\\n        if not spans_edges:\\n            continue\\n        _, n = label_mask(mask, connectivity=SPRITE_CONNECTIVITY)\\n        if n == 1:\\n            masks.append(mask)\\n    return tuple(masks)\\n\\n\\ndef _make_sprite(color: int, bg: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Sample one connected same-color sprite drawn on the background color.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    area = _randint(SPRITE_AREA_RANGE)\\n    dims = []\\n    for h, w in SPRITE_BBOXES:\\n        if h * w >= area and _valid_masks(h, w, area):\\n            dims.append((h, w))\\n    h, w = random.choice(dims)\\n    mask = random.choice(_valid_masks(h, w, area))\\n    sprite = np.full((h, w), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n    sprite[mask] = int(color)\\n    return sprite\\n\\n\\ndef _sample_palette() -> tuple[int, list[int], int | None]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Choose background, key colors, and optional distractor color.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    colors = list(COLOR_POOL)\\n    bg = random.choice(colors)\\n\\n    k = _randint(KEY_COUNT_RANGE)\\n    remaining = [c for c in colors if c != bg]\\n    key_colors = random.sample(remaining, k)\\n    remaining = [c for c in remaining if c not in key_colors]\\n    distractor_color = random.choice(remaining) if remaining and random.random() < DISTRACTOR_PROB else None\\n    return bg, key_colors, distractor_color\\n\\n\\ndef _sample_counts(k: int) -> list[int]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Sample the target outside-component count for each key color.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return [_randint(TARGET_COUNT_RANGE) for _ in range(k)]\\n\\n\\ndef _legend_shape(k: int, max_count: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return legend height and width for the key count and largest tally.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    bh = KEY_ROW_STEP * k + LEGEND_HEIGHT_TAIL\\n    bw = TALLY_COL_STEP * max_count + LEGEND_WIDTH_TAIL + _randint(LEGEND_SLACK_RANGE)\\n    return bh, bw\\n\\n\\ndef _key_row(i: int) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Map a zero-based key index to its row inside the legend/output box.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return KEY_ROW_FIRST + KEY_ROW_STEP * i\\n\\n\\ndef _build_expected(key_colors: list[int], counts: list[int]) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Render the black output tally canvas implied by key colors and counts.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    max_count = max(counts)\\n    bh, bw = _legend_shape(len(key_colors), max_count)\\n\\n    expected = np.full((bh, bw), BLACK, dtype=int)\\n    for i, (color, count) in enumerate(zip(key_colors, counts)):\\n        rr = _key_row(i)\\n        for j in range(count):\\n            expected[rr, KEY_COLUMN + TALLY_COL_STEP * j] = int(color)\\n    return expected\\n\\n\\ndef _build_legend(shape: tuple[int, int], key_colors: list[int]) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return a black legend patch with only the key-column pixels filled.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    legend = np.full(shape, BLACK, dtype=int)\\n    for i, color in enumerate(key_colors):\\n        legend[_key_row(i), KEY_COLUMN] = int(color)\\n    return legend\\n\\n\\ndef _new_grid_with_legend(bg: int, legend: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray | None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Sample a grid and paste the legend away from all canvas edges.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    bh, bw = map(int, legend.shape)\\n    H = _randint(GRID_HEIGHT_RANGE)\\n    W = _randint(GRID_WIDTH_RANGE)\\n    min_h = bh + 2 * LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN\\n    min_w = bw + 2 * LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN\\n    if H < min_h or W < min_w:\\n        return None\\n\\n    grid = np.full((H, W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n    r0 = random.randint(LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN, H - bh - LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN)\\n    c0 = random.randint(LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN, W - bw - LEGEND_EDGE_MARGIN)\\n    paste(grid, legend, r0, c0)\\n    return grid\\n\\n\\ndef _build_sprite_queue(\\n    key_colors: list[int],\\n    counts: list[int],\\n    distractor_color: int | None,\\n    bg: int,\\n) -> list[np.ndarray]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Create canonical key sprites, optional distractor sprite, and shuffle them.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    sprites = []\\n    for color, count in zip(key_colors, counts):\\n        sprite = _make_sprite(color, bg)\\n        for _ in range(count):\\n            sprites.append(sprite.copy())\\n    if distractor_color is not None:\\n        sprites.append(_make_sprite(distractor_color, bg))\\n    random.shuffle(sprites)\\n    return sprites\\n\\n\\ndef _place_sprites(grid: np.ndarray, sprites: list[np.ndarray], bg: int) -> None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Paste sprites into random non-touching free locations in queue order.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    for sprite in sprites:\\n        sr, sc = random_free_location(\\n            grid,\\n            sprite,\\n            transparent=bg,\\n            padding=SPRITE_PADDING,\\n            padding_connectivity=SPRITE_PADDING_CONNECTIVITY,\\n        )\\n        paste(grid, sprite, sr, sc, transparent=bg)\\n\\n\\ndef _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Run one output-first generation attempt and reject solver mismatches.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    bg, key_colors, distractor_color = _sample_palette()\\n    counts = _sample_counts(len(key_colors))\\n    expected = _build_expected(key_colors, counts)\\n    legend = _build_legend(expected.shape, key_colors)\\n    grid = _new_grid_with_legend(bg, legend)\\n    if grid is None:\\n        return None\\n\\n    sprites = _build_sprite_queue(key_colors, counts, distractor_color, bg)\\n    try:\\n        _place_sprites(grid, sprites, bg)\\n    except ValueError:\\n        return None\\n\\n    if not np.array_equal(main(grid), expected):\\n        return None\\n    return grid\\n\\n\\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\n    for _ in range(GENERATION_ATTEMPTS):\\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\\n        if grid is not None:\\n            return grid\\n    raise RuntimeError(\\\"failed to generate a valid 58490d8a input\\\")\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
